.TH EUCLID-CLI-EAM-CREATE-ACCESS-KEY 1 "August 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.33" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-eam-create-access-key \- create a new SigV4 access key for the logged-in user
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli
[\fB\-\-endpoint\fR \fIURL\fR]
.B eam create-access-key
.SH DESCRIPTION
Creates a new SigV4 access key for the logged-in user via the eam service's
.I create-access-key
action, and stores it locally alongside the bearer token (see
.BR euclid-cli-eam-login (1)
for the credentials file), so subsequent Euclid-service commands sign requests
with it instead of the bearer token.
.PP
The secret is only ever shown once, in this command's JSON output - if it is
lost, delete the key with
.BR euclid-cli-eam-delete-access-key (1)
and create a new one.
.PP
Self-service: any authenticated user may create their own access key, no
administrator privileges required.
.SH OPTIONS
None.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The access key was created and saved.
.TP
.B 1
The request could not be sent, or the caller is not authenticated. If the
failure was an expired or invalid session (\fIHTTP 401\fR), the stored
credentials file is removed; run
.B euclid-cli eam login
again before retrying.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli --endpoint http://localhost:5566 eam create-access-key
.fi
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $HOME/.euclid/credentials
Updated with the new \fIaccessKeyId\fR/\fIsecretAccessKey\fR on success.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-eam (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-login (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-list-access-keys (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-delete-access-key (1)
